"""Ruby and Lua: blocks/protocols versus tables, result adjustment, and embedding."""
from build import S, lesson as L

def ruby():
 return [
 L('cli','Ruby scripts and command-line conventions',
 'Ruby executes scripts directly, and -e evaluates a short program. ARGV contains user arguments without the executable name. A file can be both a reusable definition and a script by guarding its entry point with __FILE__ == $PROGRAM_NAME. Keep normal output on stdout and diagnostics on stderr. ruby -c checks syntax; it does not execute the program or prove its behavior. The tutor shows commands as text, and does not run them.',
 S('$ ruby ~-c~ app.rb','Checks syntax without normally executing the script body','Runs the application|Installs dependencies','-c requests a syntax check; BEGIN blocks are an exception and may execute.','-w|-v','What check is requested?'),
 S('# Invocation: ruby app.rb ${word}\nputs ARGV[~0~]','${word}','app.rb|nil','ARGV starts with the first user argument.','1|2','Given this invocation, what is printed?'),
 S('if __FILE__ ~==~ $PROGRAM_NAME\n puts "${word}"\nend','Runs this body when the file is the main script','Always runs on require|Defines a class','The guard separates direct execution from loading as a feature.','!=|<','What is the purpose of this guard?'),
 S('~warn~ "invalid ${word}"','Writes a diagnostic to stderr','Writes only to stdout|Terminates with failure automatically','warn reports a message without itself making the process exit unsuccessfully.','puts|p','Where does this message go?')),
 L('keywords','Keyword arguments and block contracts',
 'Ruby 3 separates keyword arguments from positional hashes. Use ** to forward a keyword hash and & to forward a block. A required keyword documents a method contract; omitting it raises ArgumentError. block_given? allows a method to offer a default behavior when no block was supplied. Lambdas enforce their declared arity more strictly than ordinary procs. Read these call boundaries before attempting to repair a missing argument.',
 S('def copies(count:); count; end\noptions={count: ${n}}\nputs copies(~**options~)','${n}','ArgumentError|nil','Double splat forwards the hash as keyword arguments.','options|*options'),
 S('def copies(count:); count; end\nputs ~copies()~','Raises ArgumentError: missing keyword count','nil|0','count: is required; an empty call cannot satisfy that contract.','copies(count: ${n})|copies(count: 0)','What happens at the call?'),
 S('def run\n block_given? ? yield : ${n}\nend\nputs ~run~','${n}','nil|LocalJumpError','The method uses its fallback because no block is supplied.','run { 0 }|run { 1 }'),
 S('f=->(x){x+1}\nputs f.call(~${n}~)','${next}','${n}|ArgumentError','The lambda receives its required argument.','|${n}, ${m}')),
 L('patterns','Pattern matching and destructuring contracts',
 'case/in matches structure and binds parts of a value. Array patterns describe positions; hash patterns normally require the named keys while permitting extra keys. **nil rejects extra keys. A pin such as ^expected compares with an existing variable instead of creating a new binding. Rightward assignment with => requires a match and raises NoMatchingPatternError otherwise. Use patterns to validate the shape of data before reading it.',
 S('case ["${word}",${n}]\nin [String => name, ~Integer => count~]\n puts count\nend','${n}','${word}|NoMatchingPatternError','The pattern checks each type and binds the integer to count.','String => count|nil'),
 S('expected=${n}\ncase ${n}\nin ~^expected~\n puts "match"\nelse\n puts "other"\nend','match','other|nil','The pin compares with the existing expected value.','nil|0'),
 S('case {name:"${word}",size:${n}}\nin {~name:~}\n puts name\nend','${word}','NoMatchingPatternError|nil','The hash pattern extracts name and permits the additional size key.','missing:|name: nil'),
 S('value=[${n}]\nvalue => ~[first, second]~','Raises NoMatchingPatternError','second becomes nil|Returns false','Rightward assignment requires two elements in this exact array pattern.','[first]|[first, *rest]','What happens when the shape does not match?')),
 L('text','Regular expressions and text-processing scripts',
 'Ruby regular expressions return MatchData or nil; named captures make extraction readable. match? tests without producing a MatchData result for the caller. sub replaces the first match, gsub replaces all matches, and the bang versions mutate their receiver. A bang method may return nil when nothing changed, so chaining it can fail. chomp removes record separators, while strip removes surrounding whitespace. Keep these different transformations explicit in data-cleaning scripts.',
 S('match=/size=(?<count>\\d+)/.match("size=${n}")\nputs match[~:count~]','${n}','nil|size','The named capture extracts digit text from the match.',':missing|0'),
 S('puts "${word} ${word}".~gsub~("${word}","X")','X X','X ${word}|${word} X','gsub replaces both occurrences.','sub|delete'),
 S('text="${word}"\np text.~gsub!~("!","?")','nil','"${word}"|false','The mutating method returns nil because it made no replacement.','gsub|upcase!'),
 S('puts /\\A\\d+\\z/.~match?~("${n}")','true','false|${n}','The anchors require the whole string to contain one or more digits.','source|names')),
 L('protocols','Duck typing and Ruby object protocols',
 'Ruby code often asks whether an object responds to a useful message rather than requiring a particular class. each is the core protocol behind Enumerable; include adds its instance methods. When no block is supplied, enum_for can expose an Enumerator instead. to_s is a display conversion while inspect targets diagnostics. respond_to? helps inspect a public capability, but cannot guarantee that a method will succeed for every input.',
 S('class Batch\n include Enumerable\n def each\n  return ~enum_for(:each)~ unless block_given?\n  yield ${n}\n end\nend\np Batch.new.to_a','[${n}]','[]|NoMethodError','Enumerable uses each; enum_for supplies deferred enumeration without a block.','nil|[]'),
 S('class Label\n def to_s; "${word}"; end\nend\nputs ~Label.new~','${word}','nil|Label','puts uses the object string representation.','Label|nil'),
 S('value="${word}"\np value.~respond_to?~(:upcase)','true','false|nil','Strings expose the public upcase method.','instance_of?|equal?'),
 S('class Batch\n include Enumerable\n def each; yield ${n}; yield ${m}; end\nend\nputs Batch.new.~count~','2','${add}|0','Enumerable derives count from the two yielded elements.','first|sum')),
 L('testing','Standard-library tests and recoverable boundaries',
 'A useful script keeps transformation methods separate from command-line input and output. Ruby ships standard-library tools such as JSON, while Test::Unit is distributed as a bundled gem on common Ruby installations and may need installation separately. These lessons read tests without requiring a testing framework in the tutor. An assertion checks an expected outcome; a test for an exception should identify the specific failure. Rescue at a boundary where the application can decide how to respond.',
 S('require "test/unit"\nclass SizeTest < Test::Unit::TestCase\n def test_add\n  ~assert_equal~ ${next}, ${n}+1\n end\nend','Passes when run with Test::Unit available','Fails because addition is invalid|Never discovers the method','A test_ method compares the expected and actual values.','assert_nil|assert_false','What is the test outcome?'),
 S('require "test/unit"\nclass SizeTest < Test::Unit::TestCase\n def test_invalid\n  ~assert_raise~(ArgumentError) { Integer("bad") }\n end\nend','Passes when run with Test::Unit available','Requires every exception type|Ignores the block','The assertion expects ArgumentError from invalid integer conversion.','assert_nothing_raised|assert_nil','What does the test establish?'),
 S('require "json"\nvalue=JSON.parse(\'{"count":${n}}\')\nputs value[~"count"~]','${n}','nil|0','JSON object keys are strings by default.',':count|0'),
 S('require "json"\nbegin\n JSON.parse("bad")\nrescue ~JSON::ParserError~\n puts "invalid"\nend','invalid','bad|Exception escapes','Rescue the parser failure at the input boundary.','ArgumentError|NoMethodError'))]

def lua():
 return [
 L('runtime','Lua chunks, globals, and embedded execution',
 'Lua runs chunks that may return values to their caller. In embedded applications the host supplies globals and decides when chunks run; do not assume a browser, operating system, or package is present. Local bindings avoid accidentally sharing state through the environment. load compiles text into a function or returns an error; compiling does not execute that function. This is a language mechanism, not a recommendation to execute untrusted text.',
 S('local fn=assert(load("return ${n}+1"))\nprint(~fn()~)','${next}','${n}|function','Calling the loaded function executes the chunk and receives its return value.','fn|0'),
 S('local fn,err=load("local =")\nprint(fn ~==~ nil)','true','false|A chunk executes','Invalid syntax makes load return nil and an error message.','~=|>'),
 S('local env={count=${n}}\nlocal fn=assert(load("return count", "config", "t", ~env~))\nprint(fn())','${n}','nil|0','The supplied environment resolves count when the chunk runs.','{}|{count=0}'),
 S('local count=${n}\ndo local count=${m} end\nprint(~count~)','${n}','${m}|nil','The inner local shadows the outer binding only within its block.','0|nil')),
 L('results','Multiple results, varargs, and nil holes',
 'Lua adjusts a function call to one result except where the final expression in a list may expand. Parenthesizing a call forces one result. Assignment fills missing values with nil and discards excess values. Varargs use ...; table.pack records their exact count in n, including nil holes. The length operator on a table with holes is not a reliable count of supplied arguments. Use table.unpack with explicit bounds when preserving those results.',
 S('local function pair() return ${n},${m} end\nlocal a,b=~pair()~\nprint(b)','${m}','nil|${n}','The final call expands to both assignment results.','(pair())|${n}'),
 S('local function pair() return ${n},${m} end\nlocal a,b=~(pair())~\nprint(b)','nil','${m}|${n}','Parentheses reduce the call to one result; b is filled with nil.','pair()|${n},${m}'),
 S('local args=table.~pack~(${n},nil,${m})\nprint(args.n)','3','2|1','pack stores the supplied argument count independently of holes.','concat|unpack'),
 S('local function count(...) return select(~"#"~,...) end\nprint(count(${n},nil,${m}))','3','2|nil','select with # counts all arguments, including nil.','1|2')),
 L('iteration','Sequence boundaries and iterator protocols',
 'A Lua table can contain both array-like and map-like entries. ipairs visits consecutive integer keys starting at one and stops at the first absent entry; pairs traverses keys without a guaranteed order. A generic for consumes an iterator protocol rather than indexing by itself. Never use # to count arbitrary map entries or tables with holes. rawget inspects stored entries without consulting __index, which is useful when reading prototype-style objects.',
 S('local values={[1]=${n},[3]=${m}}\nlocal count=0\nfor i,v in ~ipairs~(values) do count=count+1 end\nprint(count)','1','2|3','ipairs stops at the missing second element.','pairs|next'),
 S('local values={name="${word}",size=${n}}\nlocal count=0\nfor k,v in ~pairs~(values) do count=count+1 end\nprint(count)','2','0|1','pairs visits both fields, without promising their order.','ipairs|next'),
 S('local values=setmetatable({}, {__index={size=${n}}})\nprint(~rawget~(values,"size"))','nil','${n}|0','rawget bypasses the fallback and finds no stored size field.','getmetatable|type'),
 S('local function values(_,previous)\n if previous<2 then return previous+1 end\nend\nlocal count=0\nfor i in values,nil,~0~ do count=count+1 end\nprint(count)','2','1|0','The iterator receives the previous control value and stops when it returns nil.','1|2')),
 L('patterns','Lua patterns and UTF-8 boundaries',
 'Lua patterns are a compact language with % escapes; they are not JavaScript or Perl regular expressions. Captures extract substrings, gsub returns both the transformed text and replacement count, and gmatch produces an iterator. Lua strings are byte sequences: # counts bytes. The utf8 library counts Unicode code points for valid UTF-8, not user-perceived grapheme clusters. Plain string.find can disable pattern interpretation for literal searches.',
 S('local text="size=${n}"\nprint(string.match(text,~"%d+"~))','${n}','nil|size','%d+ captures the run of decimal digits.','"%a+"|"%s+"'),
 S('local text,count=string.~gsub~("${word} ${word}","${word}","X")\nprint(count)','2','1|0','gsub returns the replacement count as its second result.','match|find'),
 S('local text="λ${word}"\nprint(~utf8.len(text)~)','${unicodechars}','${unicodebytes}|1','utf8.len counts code points in this valid text; lambda occupies two UTF-8 bytes.','#text|string.len(text)'),
 S('local start=string.find("a.b",~".",1,true~)\nprint(start)','2','1|nil','The final true requests a literal dot, not the any-character pattern.','"."|"x",1,true')),
 L('resources','Closing resources and reporting failures',
 'Lua APIs commonly return nil plus an error message. pcall instead returns a success boolean followed by results or the error object. Distinguish these conventions when reading a call. Lua 5.4 to-be-closed locals use <close>; their __close metamethod runs at scope exit, including error unwinding. A closable object must satisfy that protocol, or be nil/false. File handles support it, allowing explicit lifetime boundaries in scripts and embedded hosts.',
 S('local ok,message=~pcall~(function() error("invalid") end)\nprint(ok)','false','true|nil','pcall converts the raised error into a false status and an error object.','xpcall|assert'),
 S('local closed=false\ndo\n local resource ~<close>~ = setmetatable({}, {__close=function() closed=true end})\nend\nprint(closed)','true','false|nil','The close metamethod runs when the local leaves scope.','<const>|'),
 S('local handle,message=io.open("missing.txt","r")\nif handle ~==~ nil then print("failed") end','failed','Nothing|An exception always escapes','io.open returns nil plus an error message on this assumed failure.','~=|>','Assume the file does not exist: what is printed?'),
 S('local value <const> = ${n}\n~value=${m}~','Compile error: assignment to const local','${m}|Runtime nil','A const local cannot be assigned a new value.','local value=${m}|print(value)','What does Lua reject?')),
 L('embedding','Metamethods, modules, and host boundaries',
 'Lua often provides configuration or extension logic inside a host application. Modules usually return a table of functions and keep implementation state local. require caches a loaded module in package.loaded. __call makes a table callable; __newindex handles assignments to absent keys, while writes to existing keys normally bypass it. These mechanics explain lightweight object systems without imposing a class hierarchy from another language. Host APIs and sandbox guarantees depend on the embedding application.',
 S('local item=setmetatable({}, {__call=function(self,n) return n+1 end})\nprint(~item(${n})~)','${next}','${n}|Runtime error','__call supplies behavior when this table is called like a function.','item[${n}]|item.n'),
 S('local writes=0\nlocal item=setmetatable({size=1},{__newindex=function(t,k,v) writes=writes+1; rawset(t,k,v) end})\nitem.~size~=${n}\nprint(writes)','0','1|${n}','Updating an existing raw key does not invoke __newindex.','name|other'),
 S('package.preload["lesson_config"]=function() return {size=${n}} end\nlocal a=require("lesson_config")\nlocal b=~require("lesson_config")~\nprint(a==b)','true','false|nil','require reuses the cached table for this module name.','{}|{size=${n}}'),
 S('local function create()\n local n=0\n return function() n=n+1; return n end\nend\nlocal a=create(); local b=create(); a()\nprint(~b()~)','1','2|0','Each factory call creates a distinct captured upvalue.','a()|0'))]
